INTERPRETATION (read before citing this log):
  A seed mismatch at count 0 means the two KAT procedures differ, not
  necessarily that the implementation is wrong. liboqs kat_kem/kat_sig
  use the PQC-submission-era NIST AES-256-CTR-DRBG (entropy 0..47);
  the .rsp files here are the FIPS 203/204 deterministic KATs, generated
  by a different procedure. This was verified independently: a fresh
  Python re-implementation of SP 800-90A CTR-DRBG (AES via openssl)
  reproduces liboqs's count-0 seed 06155023... byte-exact, while the
  .rsp count-0 seed 23f1c88b... is not in that DRBG stream.
  The decisive implementation check is kat-derand-check.log: fed the
  .rsp's own d||z/msg inputs through the derand API, liboqs reproduces
  the .rsp pk/sk/ct/ss. If that log shows PASS, the implementation
  matches the official FIPS vectors and only the KAT *procedure* differs.
